(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 51-year-old man has 5 weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss, and pulmonary tuberculosis is suspected. Which sputum collection plan is appropriate?","choices":["Three samples, 1 each week for 3 weeks","Three samples 8 to 24 hours apart, 1 early morning","One sample, collected at any time of day","Two samples, collected 1 hour apart in clinic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Suspected pulmonary tuberculosis calls for 3 sputum specimens collected 8 to 24 hours apart, with at least one early-morning specimen, sent for acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, with a rapid molecular test on at least one. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college student has had episodes of wheezing, chest tightness and cough for 2 years, worse at night and after cleaning his dusty dorm room. He has allergic rhinitis and atopic dermatitis (eczema). Between episodes his lung exam is normal. Spirometry shows a reduced forced expiratory volume in 1 second that improves 18% after albuterol. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Extrinsic asthma","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Vocal fold dysfunction","Intrinsic asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Episodic wheeze, chest tightness and cough worse at night, a dust trigger, allergic rhinitis and eczema (the atopic triad), a normal exam between episodes, and an 18% rise in the 1-second volume after albuterol (12% or more is a positive response) make allergic, extrinsic asthma the diagnosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 25, 35, 53"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma is given a peak flow meter to assess her control at home. When should she take her readings?","choices":["Just when she is wheezing","Once a month at the clinic","At bedtime after exercise","Each morning and each afternoon"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Peak flow is measured in the morning before the bronchodilator and in the afternoon after it. A 20% change between those readings, or from day to day, indicates poor control, so this schedule lets her and her clinician see variation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which factors select the empiric antibiotic regimen for hospital-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Whether onset was early or late in the stay","Nursing-home residence before admission","Local antibiogram and patient resistance risk","The color of the patient's sputum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, empiric therapy follows the local antibiogram and the patient's own resistance risks, with prior cultures and recent antibiotics. A common backbone is an antipseudomonal beta-lactam such as cefepime or piperacillin-tazobactam, chosen by that local data. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 39, 42, 46, 126"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman who had a kidney transplant takes long-term prednisone and anti-rejection drugs. She has 10 days of progressive shortness of breath and a dry cough, and her chest radiograph shows diffuse bilateral perihilar infiltrates. Induced sputum microscopy and Pneumocystis molecular testing are negative, but suspicion remains high. Which test should be done next?","choices":["Bronchoalveolar lavage","Expectorated sputum sample","Serum lactate dehydrogenase","Pulmonary function testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A negative induced specimen does not exclude Pneumocystis, and bronchoalveolar lavage is considered when induced sputum is negative and suspicion remains high. Her transplant, steroid use and perihilar infiltrates keep suspicion high, and the lavage sample goes for microscopy or molecular testing and other infections. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 61, 67, 69"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia has a PSI (Pneumonia Severity Index) total of 104 points. Which risk class does that total fall in?","choices":["Class II","Class III","Class IV","Class V"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. On the interpretation table, 91 to 130 points is Class IV, with a mortality of 9.3%. The index plus clinical judgment is the preferred support for the admission decision, and a total just over 100 already carries roughly a 1 in 10 risk of death. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 30, 31"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with myasthenia gravis is hypoventilating, and her carbon dioxide is rising. Following water + carbon dioxide <-> carbonic acid <-> hydrogen ion + bicarbonate, what happens to her hydrogen ion concentration?","choices":["It falls, raising her pH","It rises, lowering her pH","It is unchanged; bicarbonate absorbs it","It rises once the kidneys respond"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Added carbon dioxide drives the carbonic acid reaction to the right, forming more carbonic acid and then more hydrogen ion and bicarbonate, so pH falls: hypoventilation, raised carbon dioxide, acidemia. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man gets hives whenever he eats eggs and asks whether he can receive this season's influenza vaccine. He has never reacted to an influenza vaccine. What is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Routine vaccination and safety steps","An egg-free product","Skip influenza vaccination","Vaccinate under specialist supervision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Egg allergy does not require an egg-free product or precautions beyond routine vaccine safety measures. He has never reacted to an influenza vaccine, so he receives an age- and health-appropriate vaccine this season like any other adult. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 6"},{"q":"Which physical findings may accompany extrinsic (allergic) asthma?","choices":["Butterfly rash, arthritis, and fever","Swollen nasal mucosa, eczema, and nasal polyps","Cyanosis, edema, and distended neck veins","Clubbing, crackles, and weight loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Extrinsic, allergic asthma may bring swelling of the nasal mucosa, increased secretions, atopic dermatitis or eczema, and nasal polyps, even when the chest exam is clear between attacks. These reflect the atopic triad of rhinitis, asthma and dermatitis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Long-term supplemental oxygen is indicated when the resting arterial oxygen pressure falls below which value?","choices":["55 mmHg","65 mmHg","75 mmHg","85 mmHg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Supplemental oxygen is indicated when the arterial oxygen pressure is below 55 mmHg or the saturation below 88%, and it is proven to decrease the risk of death, prolong life and improve quality of life. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with a normal-gap metabolic acidosis from a ureteral diversion has a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). By the Winter formula, what carbon dioxide range is expected if she is compensating appropriately?","choices":["19 to 23 mm Hg","20 to 24 mm Hg","27 to 31 mm Hg","36 to 44 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 14 + 8 = 21 + 8 = 29, plus or minus 2, so 27 to 31 mm Hg. The formula needs only the bicarbonate, whatever the anion gap is or its cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who races pigeons has 6 days of fever, severe headache and a dry cough, with a lower-lobe infiltrate. A respiratory nucleic acid test detects Chlamydia psittaci. He has no drug allergies. Which treatment is preferred?","choices":["Azithromycin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Amoxicillin","Doxycycline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bird exposure, severe headache, a dry cough and a lower-lobe infiltrate with Chlamydia psittaci on a respiratory nucleic acid test is psittacosis. Doxycycline is preferred in adults, started promptly and continued about 10 to 14 days after the fever resolves to prevent relapse. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 43, 71, 81, 85, 88"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with long-standing asthma has had several exacerbations this year despite good inhaler technique and compliance. Testing shows a strong hypersensitivity to Aspergillus fumigatus with markedly raised immunoglobulin E and immunoglobulin G antibodies to the mold. Which diagnosis explains his worsening?","choices":["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Occupational asthma","Non-compliance with the controller","Steroid-resistant asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus that occurs almost exclusively in asthma or cystic fibrosis; exposure causes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response that produces acute asthma symptoms and exacerbations, as in this patient. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman in a severe asthma exacerbation sits leaning forward with hunched shoulders and uses her accessory muscles. Loud wheezing earlier has faded, and now she has only decreased breath sounds with a prolonged expiration. What does the loss of wheezing most likely indicate?","choices":["A response to her inhaler","Vocal fold dysfunction","Resolution of the exacerbation","Severe airflow obstruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Wheezing may not be present in severe exacerbations, which may show only decreased breath sounds with prolonged expiration. With her accessory muscle use and posture, a quiet chest signals worse obstruction, not improvement. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"Which test result separates emphysema from chronic bronchitis and asthma?","choices":["Reduced diffusing capacity","Bronchodilator response under 12%","Ratio below 0.70","Raised serum bicarbonate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diffusing capacity is reduced in emphysema, because destroyed alveoli and bullae limit gas transfer, separating it from chronic bronchitis (normal or near normal) and asthma. Spirometry alone cannot tell emphysema from chronic bronchitis. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 28"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease cannot generate a strong inhalation, and his rheumatoid arthritis makes it hard to load and handle his current dry powder inhaler. Which device is most appropriate for him?","choices":["Stopping inhaled therapy","Daily oral corticosteroids","Metered-dose inhaler with a spacer","A different dry powder inhaler"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Arthritis and low peak inspiratory flow limit dry powder inhalers in older adults, so a pressurized metered-dose inhaler with a spacer, a soft mist inhaler or a nebulizer is considered. The spacer reduces the need for a forceful, well-timed breath. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which electrolyte change may accompany chronic respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Hypochloremia","Hyperchloremia","Hypocalcemia","Hypernatremia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In chronic respiratory acidosis the kidneys excrete acid as ammonium chloride, so serum chloride may fall (hypochloremia) while bicarbonate rises. Chloride leaves with the acid the kidney excretes. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What change between morning and afternoon peak flow readings indicates poor asthma control?","choices":["5%","10%","20%","50%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A 20% change between morning and afternoon peak flow measurements, or a 20% change from day to day, indicates poor asthma control and should prompt a review of the treatment plan. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What fraction of inspired oxygen does room air provide?","choices":["16%","21%","24%","28%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Room air provides a fraction of inspired oxygen of .21, or 21%. That is the baseline from which every oxygen device adds, as in the nasal cannula's 24% at 1 L/min and 28% at 2 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with bronchiectasis has a year of chronic cough with sputum, fatigue and weight loss. Computed tomography shows nodular bronchiectasis, alternative diagnoses have been excluded, and two sputum cultures grow Mycobacterium avium complex. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nontuberculous mycobacterial disease","Colonization without disease","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis","Pulmonary tuberculosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacterium avium complex is a common cause of nontuberculous lung disease, and bronchiectasis raises the risk. She meets every diagnostic domain: compatible symptoms and imaging (nodular bronchiectasis), exclusion of alternatives, and 2 or more sputum cultures with the same species. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 39"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with no known tuberculosis risk factors has a tuberculin skin test for a school requirement. At 48 hours a trained reader measures 12 mm of transverse induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Negative; her cut-off is 15 mm","Positive; her cut-off is 5 mm","Positive; her cut-off is 10 mm","Indeterminate; repeat in 8 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With no known tuberculosis risk factors, her cut-off is 15 mm, the highest of the three thresholds. 12 mm of induration is below 15 mm, so the result is negative for her, even though it would be positive for someone in the 5 mm or 10 mm groups. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 22"},{"q":"What does a positive acid-fast smear of sputum establish?","choices":["Confirmed Mycobacterium tuberculosis","An immune response to infection","A group of organisms, not the species","Drug susceptibility of the organism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An acid-fast smear identifies a group of organisms, the acid-fast bacilli with lipid-rich, mycolic acid cell walls. It does not distinguish Mycobacterium tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria, so culture and molecular testing must name the species. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with acute bronchitis was managed with supportive care 10 days ago and at first improved. She now returns with a new temperature of 102.2°F, shortness of breath and crackles at the left base. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chest radiograph for pneumonia","Empiric bronchitis antibiotic","Reassurance about cough length","Codeine cough syrup"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Worsening after initial improvement, fever and dyspnea are return precautions, and fever or focal findings prompt evaluation for pneumonia rather than empiric bronchitis antibiotics. A worsening cough means reconsidering the diagnosis, and suspected pneumonia is confirmed with chest radiography. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 20, 29"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has a pH of 7.30 (normal 7.35 to 7.45) and a carbon dioxide of 52 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). To decide whether this respiratory problem is acute or chronic, which value is compared with its expected level?","choices":["Anion gap","Bicarbonate","Carbon dioxide","Urine chloride"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For a respiratory disorder, compensation is renal, so the expected bicarbonate is checked. A small rise (about 1 per 10 mm Hg) means acute, and a large rise (about 3.5 per 10) means chronic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has an exacerbation with increased, purulent sputum. A sputum culture during her last admission grew Pseudomonas aeruginosa. She is stable for outpatient care. Which oral antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Doxycycline","Levofloxacin","Azithromycin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her increased, purulent sputum meets the indication for antibiotics, and a previous Pseudomonas culture creates pseudomonal concern. Levofloxacin is the oral choice for pseudomonal concern, a respiratory fluoroquinolone that also covers atypical, gram-positive and gram-negative organisms. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 62"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman is admitted to a general ward with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia. She has never had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas isolated and has had no recent intravenous antibiotics. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Vancomycin plus cefepime","Piperacillin-tazobactam","Amoxicillin as a single drug","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nonsevere inpatient pneumonia without resistant-organism risk is treated with a beta-lactam such as ceftriaxone or ampicillin-sulbactam plus a macrolide, or with respiratory fluoroquinolone monotherapy. She has no resistant-organism history, so no broader coverage is added. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman is being discharged after a hospitalization for an exacerbation. Her blood eosinophil count is elevated. Which action belongs in her discharge bundle?","choices":["Hold inhalers until follow-up","Delay rehabilitation for 6 months","Continue oral steroids indefinitely","Start long-acting bronchodilators promptly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The discharge bundle begins long-acting bronchodilators promptly, considers an inhaled corticosteroid if eosinophils are elevated (as hers are), checks inhaler technique, considers pulmonary rehabilitation, arranges follow-up and explains the recovery timeline. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 55"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman admitted 5 days ago for a hip fracture develops fever, purulent sputum and a new infiltrate. She is not intubated and has no risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, no resistance risk and no high mortality risk. The hospital antibiogram shows most local Pseudomonas isolates are resistant to piperacillin-tazobactam but susceptible to cefepime. Which empiric agent is most appropriate?","choices":["Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Cefepime plus vancomycin","Piperacillin-tazobactam","Cefepime"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonia that begins 48 hours or more after admission is hospital-acquired. With no major resistance or high mortality risk, one antipseudomonal agent with methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus coverage is chosen using the local antibiogram, and hers favors cefepime over piperacillin-tazobactam. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 42, 44, 46"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease comes for a routine visit in October. She has not had any vaccines in the past 5 years. Which vaccines are due now?","choices":["Wait until her next exacerbation","Influenza and pneumococcal vaccines","Pneumococcal vaccine alone","Influenza vaccine alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every patient should get a yearly influenza shot, proven to reduce hospitalizations and death, and every patient should receive the pneumococcal vaccine at age 65 to reduce community-acquired pneumonia. At 66 and unvaccinated, she needs both. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 7 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1. He has had no exacerbations in the past year. Under the current three-group scheme, which group is he in?","choices":["Group E","Group B","Group C","Group A"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 7 (under 10) and a dyspnea grade of 1 (0 to 1) mean fewer symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk. Low symptoms with low risk is group A, which starts with a single bronchodilator. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman develops severe hypoxia, saturation 79%, from pneumonia. She is alert, breathing at a steady rate with good chest rise, and her arterial carbon dioxide is normal. Which device delivers the high fraction she needs?","choices":["Simple mask","Bag-valve mask","Nasal cannula","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation of 79% is severe hypoxia, yet she is alert, breathing steadily with good chest rise, so she is still ventilating well. The non-rebreather mask is the device for severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well, giving 60-100% at 10-15 L/min through its reservoir bag. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has hypertension that is hard to control and muscle weakness. He takes no diuretics, has not vomited, and eats a bag of black licorice every day. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is high. Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["His daily black licorice","Hidden vomiting","Anxiety with hyperventilation","Antacid use"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a high bicarbonate is metabolic alkalosis; the high urine chloride makes it saline unresponsive, and his high blood pressure puts it in the hypertensive group, which lists licorice. Low potassium with weakness fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college student has just been prescribed albuterol for newly diagnosed asthma. Which side effects should she be told to expect?","choices":["Oral thrush and hoarseness","Bruising and truncal weight gain","Insomnia and acid reflux","Muscle tremor and palpitations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Short-acting beta-2 agonists such as albuterol relax airway smooth muscle for about 3 to 6 hours, and their side effects include muscle tremors and palpitations. Knowing this keeps a new user from stopping her rescue inhaler out of alarm. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a stable chronic respiratory acidosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 60 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which serum electrolyte finding is expected?","choices":["High chloride","Low bicarbonate","An elevated anion gap","Low chloride"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In chronic respiratory acidosis the kidneys excrete acid as ammonium chloride and retain bicarbonate, so hypochloremia accompanies the raised bicarbonate of 33. This is the renal compensation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease was started on daily oral prednisone by another clinic 6 months ago as maintenance therapy. He has had no recent exacerbation. Which part of his regimen should be stopped or avoided?","choices":["Maintenance oral corticosteroids","An albuterol rescue inhaler","A yearly influenza vaccine","A long-acting antimuscarinic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral corticosteroids are for acute exacerbations only in this disease, which is why they are missing from the list of common chronic therapies. Maintenance is built from albuterol as needed and long-acting inhaled bronchodilators, with an inhaled corticosteroid only when indicated. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with asthma and nasal polyps developed wheezing, facial flushing and a runny nose within an hour of taking aspirin last year. She now has painful knee osteoarthritis and asks for an anti-inflammatory pain reliever. Which drug is the most appropriate choice?","choices":["Indomethacin","Naproxen","Diclofenac","Celecoxib"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her wheeze, flushing and rhinorrhea after aspirin mark aspirin-sensitive asthma, so non-selective anti-inflammatory drugs must be avoided. Selective agents such as celecoxib or meloxicam may be used, which treats her knee pain without the cross-reaction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man is found unresponsive at a party with pinpoint pupils and a respiratory rate of 6. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.24 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 62 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.24 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 62 is high, which matches it, so the problem is respiratory. Bicarbonate rose only about 1 per 10 mm Hg (22 above 40 gives about 26), the acute response. A slow rate of 6 fits central nervous system depression. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with cirrhosis and hepatic failure also has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin and takes an antacid for heartburn. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which of his conditions best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["His metformin","His antacid","Ketoacidosis from his diabetes","His hepatic failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a low carbon dioxide of 31 and a nearly normal bicarbonate is respiratory alkalosis. Hepatic failure is a listed cause of respiratory alkalosis; neither of his medicines lowers carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with severe pneumonia is on an aerosol mask from an air-entrainment nebulizer. The team wants to raise its fraction to 0.80. Why is that setting a concern?","choices":["It cannot go above 0.28","It is recommended above 0.50","It changes with his breathing rate","It is not recommended above 0.50"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A fixed-fraction air-entrainment nebulizer can deliver higher fractions, but setting above 0.50 is not recommended. His team wants 0.80, which is beyond that ceiling, so it is a concern even though his pneumonia is severe. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease comes for a routine visit, at her baseline. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 58 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Acute respiratory acidosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her carbon dioxide of 58 is high and her pH of 7.34 is just below normal, so the process is respiratory acidosis. Bicarbonate is 32, a rise of about 6 (1.8 x 3.5), the size of chronic compensation. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is a classic chronic cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman who rehearses with a church choir indoors for hours each week is diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis. She asks how she could have spread it to other choir members. Which describes how tuberculosis spreads in this setting?","choices":["Shared cups and hymn books","Infectious airborne aerosols","Brief contact while outdoors","Hand contact during services"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary and laryngeal tuberculosis release infectious aerosols during coughing, speaking or singing, and risk rises with prolonged shared indoor air and poor ventilation. A choir rehearsing indoors for hours fits that pattern. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.48 and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 26% predicted. Which airflow grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With the ratio below 0.70, a post-bronchodilator 1-second volume of 26% predicted is below 30%, grade 4, very severe. Grade 3 covers 30% to 49%. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman has mild breathlessness she attributes to deconditioning. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of 0.68 with a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 85% predicted. Which consideration applies before labeling her with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["She needs triple inhaled therapy","Any ratio below 0.70 settles it","The fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose","She needs alpha-1 antitrypsin testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In adults 65 and older, the fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose, so clinical context is weighed. A borderline ratio with near-normal airflow and mild symptoms in an 84-year-old calls for that judgment. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman who is 20 weeks pregnant and has diabetes tests positive for both influenza and coronavirus disease 2019 on a multiplex test. She has mild symptoms that began yesterday and a normal oxygen saturation. Which antiviral should be avoided because of her pregnancy?","choices":["Remdesivir","Oseltamivir","Molnupiravir","Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Molnupiravir is an adult alternative used only when the preferred coronavirus treatments are unsuitable, and it is avoided during pregnancy. Her pregnancy makes it the drug to avoid, while the influenza is treated with oseltamivir, which is preferred in pregnancy. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 104, 108, 120"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with dysphagia after a stroke is admitted to a general ward with nonsevere aspiration pneumonia and started on ceftriaxone plus azithromycin. He has never had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas isolated. On day 2, imaging newly shows a pleural empyema. Which change to his antibiotics is appropriate?","choices":["Add anaerobic coverage","Stop antibiotics; this is pneumonitis","Add vancomycin","No change to the regimen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspiration pneumonia is treated with the community or hospital regimen for its setting and severity, and anaerobic coverage is added only when abscess or empyema is suspected, not routinely. The new empyema is that trigger; drainage is also considered when indicated. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 40, 50, 51, 126"},{"q":"Four adults have community-acquired pneumonia, are stable for outpatient treatment and have no risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas. Which one fits amoxicillin as a single drug?","choices":["The one with no chronic conditions","The one with diabetes mellitus","The one with asplenia","The one with chronic liver disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin, or doxycycline, is the outpatient choice when there are no comorbidities and no methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas risk. Each of the others has a listed comorbidity, which calls for a beta-lactam plus azithromycin or doxycycline, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman who lives in a nursing home is brought in with 2 days of fever and productive cough, and her admission chest radiograph shows a new infiltrate. She has never had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas isolated from her airway and has not recently been hospitalized for intravenous antibiotics. How should this pneumonia be classified?","choices":["Hospital-acquired pneumonia","Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Community-acquired pneumonia","Health care-associated pneumonia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Community-acquired pneumonia is acquired outside the hospital or present early in admission, and prior health care contact does not exclude it. The health care-associated category is retired, and nursing-home residence alone does not mandate broad-spectrum therapy; her lack of resistant-organism risks keeps her on the community pathway. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A healthy 29-year-old woman has acute histoplasmosis confirmed by urine antigen after clearing out a bat-infested attic. Ten days after onset her fever is resolving and her cough is improving, and she is not short of breath. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Amphotericin-based induction","Observation without an antifungal","Itraconazole for 12 months","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severity and host risk guide treatment, and mild acute disease in an immunocompetent adult often needs observation rather than antifungals. She is healthy and already improving, so neither itraconazole nor amphotericin is needed. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with asthma follows a written action plan. His personal best peak flow is 600 L/min. Today he is very short of breath, his quick-relief medicine has not helped, and his peak flow is 260 L/min. Which action-plan zone is he in?","choices":["Green zone","Red zone","Yellow zone","Between zones, so recheck tomorrow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A peak flow of 260 out of a personal best of 600 is about 43%, below 50% of personal best, and he is very short of breath despite quick-relief medicine. Those are red-zone, medical-alert findings: take the rescue medicine and call the doctor or get help now. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with group E chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is recovering from a hospitalization. She is breathless walking around her home and has never been referred for rehabilitation. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["No referral because of her age","Thoracic surgery","Pulmonary rehabilitation","Behavioral health alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary rehabilitation is essential for groups B and E and, next to smoking cessation, tends to be the most effective therapy, reducing dyspnea and readmissions. Older adults are less often referred, so she should be referred actively. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 53, 55"},{"q":"What must be done before starting treatment for latent tuberculosis infection?","choices":["Repeat the positive skin test","Wait for symptoms to appear","Obtain a negative blood assay","Exclude tuberculosis disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tuberculosis disease is excluded before latent treatment begins, because a positive skin test or blood assay shows infection but cannot separate latent infection from disease. Interactions, pregnancy and source-case resistance are also checked. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"In the CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score, which respiratory rate earns a point?","choices":["20 or more per minute","24 or more per minute","30 or more per minute","35 or more per minute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A respiratory rate of 30 or more breaths per minute scores 1 point, as do confusion, urea above 20 mg/dL (7 mmol/L), systolic pressure below 90 or diastolic 60 or less, and age 65 or older. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 31, 29"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man's chest radiograph and sputum smear lead his clinician to presume pulmonary tuberculosis; culture results are weeks away. Which reporting step is appropriate now?","choices":["Report if drug resistance is found","Notify the tuberculosis program now","No report; the clinician manages it","Report after culture confirmation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The local or state tuberculosis program is notified of presumed or confirmed tuberculosis, so reporting does not wait for culture. Public-health coordination also guides the criteria for ending isolation. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has asthma symptoms 3 days a week, wakes at night 3 times a month, and uses albuterol 3 days a week but not daily. She has minor limitation of activity. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 72% predicted with a ratio reduced by 4%. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Intermittent","Severe persistent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms, night waking, rescue use and minor limitation fit mild persistent, but a 1-second volume between 60% and 80% predicted (72%) is a moderate persistent feature, and severity follows the most severe category in which any feature occurs. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has diabetic ketoacidosis with Kussmaul respirations (deep, rapid breathing). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.19 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 21 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which approach corrects her acid-base disorder?","choices":["Paper bag rebreathing","Treat the underlying disorder","Bicarbonate until her pH is normal","Slow her breathing to retain carbon dioxide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elevated-gap metabolic acidosis is treated by treating the underlying disorder; her pH of 7.19 is acidemic but not in the unsustainable range below 6.7. Her deep breathing is compensation and is left alone. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 35, 57, 58"},{"q":"An elevated anion gap is usually due to which change?","choices":["A decrease in unmeasured anions","An increase in unmeasured anions","An increase in unmeasured cations","An increase in serum chloride"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An elevated anion gap is usually due to an increase in unmeasured anions. Less often it is due to a decrease in unmeasured cations (calcium, magnesium, potassium, gamma-globulins). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and heart failure is hospitalized on day 5 of influenza, confirmed by molecular testing, with hypoxemia. Which antiviral is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral or enteric oseltamivir","Inhaled zanamivir","Single-dose baloxavir","Amantadine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized patients generally receive oral or enteric oseltamivir, which is preferred for hospitalized, severe and high-risk patients and is started even after 48 hours. His age, lung and heart disease and admission all point to it, with renal dose adjustment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 97, 98, 108, 109"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has had stable, well-controlled asthma for 2 years. How often should his spirometry be repeated to monitor progression?","choices":["Every month","Every 3 months","Every year","Every 5 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spirometry is best done before and after a short-acting bronchodilator and is repeated annually to monitor progression. For this man with stable, controlled disease, a yearly test tracks his lung function between routine follow-up visits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man starting home oxygen asks why he must be so careful at home if oxygen is \"just air.\" Which property of oxygen underlies the safety teaching?","choices":["It is toxic to breathe for visitors","It is heavier than water","It is highly flammable","It turns into carbon monoxide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is highly flammable, which is why home oxygen teaching includes no smoking in the home, staying away from heat sources, avoiding petroleum products and posting signs. Oxygen is not just air, and fire is a listed hazard. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is ready for discharge after his treatment is optimized. His resting saturation on room air is 87%, and he has no pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease) or high hematocrit. How often will his home oxygen be recertified?","choices":["Every 1 month","Every 3 months","Every 12 months","Every 24 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His resting room-air saturation is 87%, at or below 88%, which is the Group I qualification (conditional Group II needs a comorbidity such as pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or a hematocrit above 56%). Group I patients are recertified every 12 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"}]};
